  "steps": [
   {
    "id": 661851,
    "guid": "BB4AE9CA9BCA4CCEBA42CC8CB9D03311",
    "previous_id": 0,
    "previous_guid": null,
    "section": "POOLING PREP",
    "section_color": null,
    "section_duration": 0,
    "is_substep": false,
    "step": "Transfer the libraries into a new 384 well Echo source plate. Skip if samples already complete. Make sure to dilute samples if needed to contain enough dead volume. Samples should be between 22-25uL to be able to dispense accurately using the Echo.\n\n\n\nWells of the source plate containing samples should have a dead volume of 20uL for the Echo to be able to pool accurately.\n \n",
    "data": null,
    "protocol_id": 15461,
    "case_id": 0,
    "critical_ids": "",
    "duration": 0,
    "original_id": 0,
    "number": "1",
    "cases": [],
    "critical": null
   },
   {
    "id": 661852,
    "guid": "E0E44EE6CF854944BFEE3B06F87F5967",
    "previous_id": 666394,
    "previous_guid": "C61963C0BD3111E88CE427F34D7B9B60",
    "section": "EQUAL VOLUME SEQUENCING",
    "section_color": null,
    "section_duration": 0,
    "is_substep": false,
    "step": "Sequence pool on an Illumina iSeq, or a low-throughput sequencer.",
    "data": null,
    "protocol_id": 15461,
    "case_id": 0,
    "critical_ids": "",
    "duration": 0,
    "original_id": 0,
    "number": "4",
    "cases": [],
    "critical": null
   },
   {
    "id": 661853,
    "guid": "EAC03B93CA614ED19FAB522EA38FEED6",
    "previous_id": 661852,
    "previous_guid": "E0E44EE6CF854944BFEE3B06F87F5967",
    "section": "POOLING CALCULATIONS",
    "section_color": null,
    "section_duration": 0,
    "is_substep": false,
    "step": "Using the output from equal volume pooling, determine the approximate representation of each library in the total pool.\n\n[Screen Shot 2018-08-16 at 10.53.37 AM.png]](https://content.protocols.io/7ng2jk6.png) \n_________________________________\n\nThe sum of the inverse of the percent fraction of each sample from the iSeq run should give a normalization factor. This normalization factor will be used to determine the volume of each sample to be pooled.\u00a0\n\n_** Needed information:**_\n\n1. ratio of reads of each sample to total number of reads (percent of reads) = x \n2. desired total volume of pool necessary for sequencing submission = T (uL)\n\nUse this information to solve for N (or the normalization factor). \n\n\nThe normalization factor is an estimation that can be used to pool evenly. It can be adjusted for various reasons to fit the criteria per batch.\n \n",
    "data": null,
    "protocol_id": 15461,
    "case_id": 0,
    "critical_ids": "",
    "duration": 0,
    "original_id": 0,
    "number": "5",
    "cases": [],
    "critical": null
   },
   {
    "id": 661855,
    "guid": "6A034370FBBE4A7AAE7DCA718FA3D5AF",
    "previous_id": 666692,
    "previous_guid": "1851BDD0BDC411E88CE427F34D7B9B60",
    "section": "EQUI-MOLAR POOLING",
    "section_color": null,
    "section_duration": 0,
    "is_substep": false,
    "step": "Use the Echo to dispense the original sequencing libraries to\u00a0the final pool using the calculated volumes. \n\nUse a new 384 PCR plate, or a 96 well PCR plate, as the destination plate to dispense the appropriate number of samples into the necessary number of wells. Take in account the maximum working volumes. \n\n\n\n\nReminder: For 384, do not dispense more than 11uL into each well total.\u00a0\n \n\n",
    "data": null,
    "protocol_id": 15461,
    "case_id": 0,
    "critical_ids": "",
    "duration": 0,
    "original_id": 0,
    "number": "7",
    "cases": [],
    "critical": null
   },
   {
    "id": 666393,
    "guid": "93778190BD3111E88CE427F34D7B9B60",
    "previous_id": 661851,
    "previous_guid": "BB4AE9CA9BCA4CCEBA42CC8CB9D03311",
    "section": "EQUAL VOLUME POOLING",
    "section_color": null,
    "section_duration": 0,
    "is_substep": false,
    "step": "Using the Labcyte Echo, pool 0.5uL of each library into a 384-well destination plate.\u00a0 \n\nUse a new 384 PCR plate, or a 96 well PCR plate, as the **_destination plate_** to dispense the appropriate number of samples into the necessary number of wells. Be sure to take\u00a0into account of the maximum working volume per destination plate. For example, for a 384 well plate, do not dispense more than 12uL into each well (i.e. 500nL of 22 samples per well).\u00a0",
    "data": null,
    "protocol_id": 15461,
    "case_id": 0,
    "critical_ids": "",
    "duration": 0,
    "original_id": 0,
    "number": "2",
    "cases": [],
    "critical": null
   },
   {
